CORE
main.c
--unwind 6 --rounds 3 --por --allow-pointer-unsoundness
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
Every thread picks an element a[i] and, holding a[i]'s own mutex, either
increments and decrements its datum or asserts that it is 0; the threads go
through `p = &a[i]`. No assertion can fail.

`pthread_mutex_lock(&p->mutex)` stores through a pointer at an undecided
offset, lowered to `a' == byte_update(a, offset, v)` and split into one
assignment per field of every element. Each was an unconditional shared
write carrying the value the thread had read when the statement started, so
locking a[3] republished a stale a[4].datum over another thread's update of
it, and that thread's assertion failed. Now only the fields whose bytes the
store meets are published.

Reported FAILED before the fix, SUCCESSFUL after. Reduced from goblint
28-race_reach_74-tricky_address1_racefree (which the fix corrects, with _75
and _76). It needs five elements and `--unwind 6`: with fewer elements, or
a bound that cuts the initialisation loop, it passes before the fix as well.

See `store_through_pointer_lost_update_real`: the same program without the
per-element lock, where the assertion really can fail.
